            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>David as King of Judah</strong><br>\nDavid was anointed King of Judah at Hebron after Saul's death. He ruled there for seven years and six months, establishing his reign with political skill and military strength. During this time, he united the tribe of Judah and gained support to strengthen his claim to the entire kingdom of Israel.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Unification of Israel</strong><br>\nDavid's monarchy marked the unification of the northern and southern kingdoms under a single ruler. After becoming the king of all Israel, he conquered Jerusalem and established it as the nation's capital. This action not only centralized his political power but also set the stage for Jerusalem's significance in Jewish history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Expanding the Kingdom</strong><br>\nKing David was a capable warrior and strategist, expanding the territory of Israel to its greatest extent. His military campaigns extended Israelite rule over neighboring lands, including Edom, Moab, and Ammon. David's reign brought a period of prosperity and power to Israel that set the foundation for its future.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
