          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>David's Administration</strong><br>\nTo govern his expanded territory, David organized a central administration and appointed officials. He delegated responsibilities to his sons and loyal followers, ensuring that his governance reflected a mix of family ties and meritocracy. This structure helped to manage the diverse aspects of Israelite life and maintain order.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Family Conflicts</strong><br>\nDespite his success as a king, David's personal life was fraught with conflict. His family was marked by internal strife, including the rape of his daughter Tamar by her half-brother Amnon, and the subsequent rebellion led by his son Absalom. These events brought turmoil to David's household and challenged his reign.</p>\n\n<p><strong>David's Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nKing David's legacy is multifaceted; he is remembered as a warrior, a poet, and the psalmist king who had a special covenant with God. Despite his moral failures, like his affair with Bathsheba, David was celebrated for his heart for God. He died at the age of seventy after ruling Israel for 40 years, leaving a united kingdom to his son Solomon.</p>\n"
              },
